answer:
To solve the problem, we are given a rotation centered at the origin that maps the vector  
$$
\begin{pmatrix} 13 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}
$$  
to  
$$
\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -12 \end{pmatrix}.
$$  
Our goal is to determine where this same rotation takes the vector  
$$
\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}.
$$

---

### Step 1: Understand the Rotation

A rotation in the plane can be represented by a rotation matrix of the form:
$$
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos \theta & -\sin \theta \\
\sin \theta & \cos \theta
\end{pmatrix}
$$
where $\theta$ is the angle of rotation.

Applying this matrix to the vector $\begin{pmatrix} 13 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$, we get:
$$
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos \theta & -\sin \theta \\
\sin \theta & \cos \theta
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
13 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
13 \cos \theta \\
13 \sin \theta
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
5 \\
-12
\end{pmatrix}
$$

From this, we can deduce:
$$
13 \cos \theta = 5 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \cos \theta = \frac{5}{13}
$$
$$
13 \sin \theta = -12 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \sin \theta = -\frac{12}{13}
$$

---

### Step 2: Apply the Rotation to the Vector $\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$

Now, apply the same rotation matrix to the vector $\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$:
$$
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos \theta & -\sin \theta \\
\sin \theta & \cos \theta
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
-\sin \theta \\
\cos \theta
\end{pmatrix}
$$

Substituting the values of $\sin \theta$ and $\cos \theta$:
$$
\begin{pmatrix}
-\left(-\frac{12}{13}\right) \\
\frac{5}{13}
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\frac{12}{13} \\
\frac{5}{13}
\end{pmatrix}
$$

---

### Final Answer

$$
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} \dfrac{12}{13} \\ \dfrac{5}{13} \end{pmatrix}}
$$

